\chapter{Scaffold}

Stage one needs a build tool, a test runner, a UI framework, and the two pinned
libraries. Every version is exact. A rope that behaves today must behave the
same after a dependency update, so nothing uses a caret or a tilde.

All code lives in the \texttt{app} directory, which the assembly routes to. The
book stays at the root. The package manifest is written here and tangled out.
The lock file is produced
by npm when the packages are installed, and it is committed with the manifest.

\section{Packages}

Runtime: three.js renders, Rapier simulates, React draws the page. Development:
Vite builds, Vitest tests, Tailwind styles the page (never the canvas), and
TypeScript checks types. The versions were the latest releases at scaffold time.

@d package manifest @{
{
  "name": "roppi",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "private": true,
  "description": "a game of ropes using ldd",
  "type": "module",
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "vite",
    "build": "tsc --noEmit && vite build",
    "test": "vitest run",
    "typecheck": "tsc --noEmit"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@@dimforge/rapier3d-compat": "0.21.0",
    "react": "19.3.1",
    "react-dom": "19.3.1",
    "three": "0.186.1"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@@tailwindcss/vite": "4.3.3",
    "@@types/react": "19.3.0",
    "@@types/react-dom": "19.3.0",
    "@@types/three": "0.186.0",
    "@@vitejs/plugin-react": "6.1.2",
    "tailwindcss": "4.3.3",
    "typescript": "5.9.3",
    "vite": "8.3.4",
    "vitest": "5.0.5"
  }
}
@}

\section{Build and test configuration}

Vite serves the page and builds it. The same file configures Vitest. Tests run
in plain Node, because the topology and the rope model need no browser. The
renderer is tested for its geometry, and the page itself is checked by running
it.

@d Vite configuration @{
import { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';
import react from '@@vitejs/plugin-react';
import tailwindcss from '@@tailwindcss/vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],
  test: {
    environment: 'node',
    // The rope runs four physics substeps per frame, so its tests take seconds.
    testTimeout: 20000,
    include: ['src/**/*.test.ts'],
  },
});
@}

@d TypeScript configuration @{
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["src", "vite.config.ts"]
}
@}

\section{Page shell}

The page has one root element. React mounts into it and nothing else is
written into the document.

@d HTML shell @{
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Roppi</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="root"></div>
    <script type="module" src="/src/main.tsx"></script>
  </body>
</html>
@}

@d Global styles @{
@@import "tailwindcss";

html,
body,
#root {
  height: 100%;
  margin: 0;
}
@}

@d Entry point @{
import { StrictMode } from 'react';
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
import { App } from './App';
import './index.css';

createRoot(document.getElementById('root')!).render(
  <StrictMode>
    <App />
  </StrictMode>,
);
@}
